and data management.  <I>Archaeological Method and Theory</I>, Vol. 1, ed. M.B. Schiffer, 139-203.  
Tuscon:  University of Arizona Press, 1989.<P>
<B>Kenneth L. Kvamme</B>.  One-sample tests in regional archaeological analysis:  New possibilities 
through computer technology.  <I>American Antiquity </I>55 (1990):  367-381.<P>
<B>Kenneth L. Kvamme</B>.  The use of geographic information systems for modeling 
archaeological site distributions.  <I>Geographic Informations Systems in Government</I>, Vol. 1, ed. 
Bruce K. Opitz, 345-362.  Hampton, Virginia:  A. Deepak Publishing, 1986.<P>
<B>3.  GIS AND DATABASE CONCEPTS</B><P>
<B>John C. Antenucci <I>et al</B>.</I>  <I>Geographic Information Systems:  A Guide to the 
Technology</I>.  New York:  Van Nostrand Reinhold, 1991.  (Ch. 5)<P>
<B>William E. Huxhold</B>.  <I>An introduction to Urban Geographic Information Systems</I>.  
New York:  Oxford University Press, 1991. (Ch. 2)<P>
<B>Gail Langran</B>.  <I>Time in Geographic Information Systems.  </I>London:  Taylor and 
